Tony's named attributes
=============================================================================

fit
    did:        sizes a container to the sum of its members. Value picks the
                direction: height/down, width/across, both, text; no value
                follows the container's own across/down. Down adds member
                heights and takes the widest width; across the reverse. A
                member that itself has fit is fitted first (recursive).
    where:      Details.twk getFitSettings 266-282 (reads the value, sets
                fitAcross/fitDown/fitBoth/fitText); Control.twk fitSize
                103-162 (the arithmetic); called from Control layout 259-264,
                a pass of its own between setDetails and setFrame.
                Stuff/Sheet.rtn 49-82 repeats the reading for matrix rows.
    when:       layout -- its own pass, after every field's details are set
                and before any frame is placed.
    inherited:  no (wig%fit, own attribute only).
    leans on:   member width/height already known (fixed numbers); does not
                look at text. fit=text sets fitText and NOTHING reads it --
                fitSize has no text branch, so fit=text did nothing.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant -- arithmetic over member sizes. fit=text
                would need font measure.
    lean:       keep; respell as a layout-pass action. Decide whether
                fit=text is wanted (it never worked) or dropped.

flex
    did:        almost nothing in layout: it forces across, and with down it
                writes flexDirection=column. Both are CSS flexbox words; the
                only other reader of flex is IncantForms/HTML/htmlTraits
                (display=flex, flex-direction). The layout code named "flex"
                (Control.twk flexLayout 165-240, setFlexDetails 815-833) is
                NOT driven by the flex attribute -- it is driven by
                contentVaries, which is set by across="*" or down="*" (a
                value on across/down: repeat the member template as many
                times as there is room, used with next= and a source).
    where:      Details.twk initialize 305-326. Bot/Details.twk 394-408 same.
    when:       layout (details set-up).
    inherited:  no.
    leans on:   the HTML transpiler; the templates use it on tableBody,
                tableLabel, tableRow.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant.
    lean:       drop as a layout attribute (it is an HTML hint). The real
                mechanism worth keeping is the across="*"/down="*" repeater
                -- see the across/down row. Clay's guess that flex and
                valueless space are one mechanism: NO, measured -- flex is a
                flexbox hint and the repeater fills with copies, not space.

expander (with expandable, expanded)
    did:        a tree's open/closed marker. A member tagged expander gets
                its group set to the template expanded ("-") or expandable
                ("+") depending on whether its leaf's source has members and
                whether the leaf is expanded; a leaf with no members gets an
                empty marker.
    where:      Map.rtn setExpander 803-830; called by tREE (1300) and by the
                expandTree event action xPAND (Actions.twk 399-421).
                Templates: IncantForms/Controls/templates expandable/expanded.
    when:       layout (tree set-up) and event (after each expand/collapse).
    inherited:  no; finds leaf with findAttribute (walks parents).
    leans on:   tEMPLATEs registry, the tree / leaf / source machinery,
                expandTREE and cOLLAPSErow.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant (it swaps a child's group); drawing is text.
    lean:       keep, as part of a tree form; respell as a kant action. It is
                not an attribute so much as a reserved member name.

matrix (matrixBody matrixBox matrixColumnHead ... in templates)
    did:        the drill-down / across spreadsheet: buildMatrix, expand by
                row or column, totals by sum, right-click menus to choose the
                expansion.
    where:      Stuff/Sheet.rtn (1,520 lines: buildMatrix 301, expand 587-
                740, expandThisRow/Column 899-1097, sUM 1502 ...); templates
                in IncantForms/Controls/templates.
    when:       define (templates merge), layout (build), event (expand via
                rightClick, sum).
    inherited:  matrix templates use $columnWidth $rowHeight $labelWidth
                macros, resolved where the template is referenced.
    leans on:   Source (sorting, mapping), mapWRT / BitMAP merging, rightClick,
                popup, the old #macro template facility.
    C++ floor:  BitMAP (bit ops); draw for the cells.
    lean:       ⚠ Sheet.rtn is NOT COMPILED: Bwana.twk:16 reads
                `//include Sheet.rtn` (Bot/Bwana.twk:16 the same), so matrix,
                bigify's builder and sum were already off in both copies.
                Lean: drop for first light; reassess with the sheet form and
                BitMAP.

popup
    did:        popup=Name makes a click open the window or panel Name
                (registered in windows); if not registered and the field has
                file=, it loads that file as a panel first.
    where:      Map.rtn popUP 648-655 (hangs action "display"); Actions.twk
                dISPLAY 6-53 (finds windows[name], keyFront, display).
    when:       layout sets it up; event fires it (click).
    inherited:  no.
    leans on:   the windows registry, controller.load, setAction.
    C++ floor:  window (show / bring to front).
    lean:       keep; respell as a click action that opens a named panel.

pullDown
    did:        pullDown=Name links the field to the menu block Name
                (located by name), and marks the menu with pULLdOWN pointing
                back; a click opens it via action "menu" (Actions.twk mENU
                155-222).
    where:      Map.rtn pullDOWN 660-679.
    when:       layout sets up; event fires.
    inherited:  no.
    leans on:   locate, the menu form, setAction.
    C++ floor:  draw (a menu is a drawn panel); possibly window.
    lean:       keep; respell. Likely the same mechanism as popup with a
                different target, worth one action for both.

toggled
    did:        toggled=Name puts a block into a toggle group. If the block
                also has off it starts hidden (noRoom). Its click action
                "toggle" (Actions.twk tOGGLE 362-397) locates the wrapper
                Name and flips noRoom on every block in it whose toggled
                value is Name -- show/hide by group.
    where:      Map.rtn tOGGLED 1191-1204.
    when:       layout sets up; event fires.
    inherited:  no.
    leans on:   noRoom (the hide flag layout honours), locate.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant (flip a flag, re-layout).
    lean:       keep, respell -- simple and general (show/hide groups).

key
    did:        a flag. In a sorted list, a key field whose value equals the
                same field in the previous row is shown blank (showBlank),
                so repeated values print once -- "Artist" once over its
                albums. Only meaningful together with trait.
    where:      Map.rtn flagFIELD case 'k' 346-394 (keyField); used in tRAIT
                1225-1243; honoured by Control.twk 598 and Details.rtn 93.
    when:       layout.
    inherited:  no.
    leans on:   trait, source, the previous row.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant.
    lean:       keep, respell; rename (key collides with keyboard key).

trait
    did:        binds a field's display to one attribute of the current
                source record: trait reads its own value, or the field's tag
                if none, and sets the field's group to record[that] (trait=
                source shows the whole record). It registers the field as a
                LISTENER of the source, so it updates when the source moves.
    where:      Map.rtn tRAIT 1207-1258.
    when:       layout, then again on every source change (listener).
    inherited:  finds sRCe with findAttribute (walks parents) -- so the
                source is inherited from an enclosing source= block.
    leans on:   source (the Source class, Source.twk), listeners, key.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant.
    lean:       keep; this is exactly A2's listener question (field bound to
                another field's value, updated on change).

sort
    did:        sort=attr on a table heading makes a click sort the source
                by that attribute; shift-click sorts descending.
    where:      Map.rtn setSORT 871-885 (hangs action "sORT"); Actions.twk
                sORT 333-357 (source.sort(mask.text)); Source.twk sort.
    when:       layout sets up; event fires.
    inherited:  finds sRCe with findAttribute.
    leans on:   source, Source.sort, shift-key state.
    C++ floor:  none, pure kant (sort a list); key modifier state from the
                event.
    lean:       keep, respell. Sorts the displayed source, not the data --
                as Tony's notes guessed.

keyStroke (and keyAction)
    did:        keyStroke=c-A parses a key spec with the KeyStroke rule
                (Groups.twk processKeySpec 115-131) and marks the attribute
                and its parent keyAction. On a key-up, Layout.twk keyUp walks
                from the selection up to the first block with keyAction and
                fires the attribute whose keyStrokeMatch (Map.rtn 480-494)
                matches the key and the alt/control/command/shift state.
                keyAction alone is a flag (flagFIELD case 'k').
    where:      Map.rtn keySTROKE 466-477; Groups.twk / Groups.rtn
                processKeySpec; Layout.twk 179-249.
    when:       define-ish (spec parsed at layout), event (key).
    inherited:  matching uses findAttribute.
    leans on:   the KeyStroke grammar rule (Groups.g), the event's modifier
                state, the jit (each keyStroke entry carries jit code).
    C++ floor:  key events and modifier state.
    lean:       keep; respell -- keyStroke entries are already actions with
                a key filter, which is close to kant's "every attribute can
                have an action".
